## Rule Loading Timing

Understanding *when* rules load is essential for writing rules that work as intended. This applies to all agents.

**Glob-triggered rules load when a matching file enters context — not when the agent plans its approach.** If an agent decides how to handle a task before opening any matching files, the rule arrives too late to influence that decision. This makes glob triggers the right choice for constraints on file content, but the wrong choice for rules that need to shape the agent's approach before it starts work.

**Description-matched rules load at planning time**, before any files are opened, when the agent judges the task matches the description. Use this trigger for workflow rules, review procedures, or any rule that must be in context before the agent decides its approach.

**Practical consequence — workflow rules need description-matching, not globs:**

| Intent | Correct trigger | Why |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Constrain how the agent edits a specific file type | Glob | Loads exactly when those files are touched |
| Guide how the agent approaches a task | Description-match | Loads at planning time, before files are opened |
| Apply on every request unconditionally | Always-on | Loads regardless of files or intent |

**Per-agent notes:**

- **Cursor, Windsurf, Kiro, Augment** — a single rule can carry both a description and globs; the description handles intent-matching at planning time, the globs handle file-editing coverage
- **Claude Code** — description-matching is not supported; the only triggers are `paths` (glob) and always-on. If a glob rule also needs to load at planning time, create a separate companion rule with no `paths` field and a description written to match the intent

